        "id": "lem-hard-attention-depth-reduction",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Hard-attention depth reduction under input restrictions",
        "deps": [
          "def-hahn-hard-soft-attention-sequence-model",
          "thm-asymmetric-lovasz-local-lemma"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 2,
      "statement": "Fix a finite content alphabet $\\Sigma$ with $s=|\\Sigma|\\ge2$, a hard-attention model with fixed $H\\ge1$ heads per position and $L\\ge1$ layers, and an integer $c\\ge1$. At length $n$, the model input is $(x_1,\\ldots,x_{n-1},\\mathtt{eos})$, with the end marker fixed at position $n$. An input restriction assigns each content position $i<n$ either $*$ (free) or a fixed symbol of $\\Sigma$; its free positions are precisely the content positions marked $*$. Say that a layer-zero activation, including the activation at the end-marker position, depends on at most $c$ free positions if, with the restriction fixed, its value is determined by at most $c$ of the remaining free input symbols. Suppose the current restriction leaves at least $Cn$ content positions free for every sufficiently large $n$, where $0<C\\le1$, and every layer-zero activation depends on at most $c$ free positions. For every $0<C'<C$, there are an integer $c'\\ge1$ and a refinement, for every sufficiently large $n$, that leaves at least $C'n$ content positions free and preserves the model's output on every completion, while absorbing the first remaining attention layer into new layer-zero activations that each depend on at most $c'$ original content positions. Repeating this conclusion through the fixed $L$ layers gives a restriction with at least $C'n$ free content positions on which the output depends on at most a constant number of original content positions. The constants and the length threshold may depend on the fixed model, $c,C,C'$, but not on $n$."
      },
